Z||000004||ray b||02-22-2001||03:52 PM||||I liked the QUOTE[those who don't remember  history will be condemed to repeat it]that jim jones had up at peoples temple site.<BR>but it didn't help them at all[1000 dead].<BR>but history is only written by the winners<BR>and allways shows bias of writer.like some writers only cover 6m jews killed and forget <BR>all the others like gypsy, gays, commies ect killed at same time by same evil people.<BR>most of history has been lost to us now and never will be recovered.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||02-22-2001||03:57 PM||||<BR>FieroLisa, I was not a student who majored in history while in school, but have found an interest in history since my graduation in 1996 (University Life).  I have purchased books, videos, and satellite TV to feed this beast, all of which has grown from talking with my grand father about his life.  So, I consider myself to a hobbist historian, and I find history very enlightening and interesting.<P>So, this brings me to your question what is the Value of History?  How does it have value? For anything to have value, you must have a standard against which one measures.  So what standard do we use to measure the value of history.<P>The common thought is those that do not study history are doomed to repeat it, but why do we have so many that do not want to study history?<P>The biggest problem that most people have is that they cannot see past their own lives.  Most fail to understand that things occured prior to their birth and will happen after their death, yet that everything they do has an impact on those around them, from family, to friends, to community, to law.<P>Most do not understand the foundation to that which we have today.  What those did prior to our birth chose to do regarding life in general and toward their governmental institutions.  Every generation is a test bed for humanity.  Will we allow ourselves to have freedom or allow others to rule our lifes?  Will those who have freedom, understand how to maintain it and the cost to maintain it?  Will those who are in bondage, want to break free, can they break free?<P>Life, as well as history, is made of sacrifices.  Sacrifices to self, and sacrifices to others.  Those who see the larger picture of life and humanity rarely are self-focused.  These people have given much or all they had to give for a cause greater than their own self-interest.<P>Consider the founders of the USA, they lost their wealth, health, and life to found a nation based on law, and equality.  MLK, lost his life, trying to bring color-blindness to humanity.  Those who have the greatest impact, usually lose the greatest amount during their life.<P>So what is the value of history?<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Hank is Here||02-22-2001||04:12 PM||||Lisa, the one thing I have learned is that history is writen by the winners, either politically or militarily.  History is not the truth, it is the intrepretation of past events.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||02-22-2001||04:21 PM||||History is my major.  History is cool  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">  I alway do well on my papers because I don't try to grasp the whole theme.  Instead I usually write about the topics which best support the general, broad topic and let the reader draw his own conclusions.  If you don't have one specific area or personality of history to study, your paper will be bouncing all over the place, and you won't accomplish anything.  <P>Good luck.  You'll know it's an "A" paper when you read it, I always do.  If you want, send me a copy of what you have and I could give you some tips.||||reg||1
