Z||000013||Fformula88||02-22-2001||08:16 PM||||I have a fancy sheet of paper on my wall that says I have a Bachelor of Arts degree in History.  So I hope I can add something usefull here since I am having difficulty finding other uses for that sheet of paper  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It sounds like you have a good basis there for a paper.  All three points can be argued well.  The first one seems a little difficult.  I'd say studying history does those things, to the end of becoming an critical and analytical thinker.  By studying history, people are evaluating past events based on current knowledge and experience.  <P>The second sounds like a good one.  Only by looking at what happened in the past... or by knowing where human civilization was, can we understand why it is the way it exists now.  For example, people tend to take this country for granted, but as Gold-86SE said, people sacraficed to make it the way it is.  Even this past election, and the voting process (electoral college, states rights to set voting laws) is based in History.  Many ppeople wonder why there isn't a uniform election procedure, but by looking at history they'd see that the founders were afraid of making another aristocracy like England had that would infringe on basic rights.  So they divided the power of government up between federal and state (or local) governments to keep the federal government from gaining too much power that it would infringe on rights.<P>Your third point seems to follow the "doomed to repeat it" theory, which is always a good argument.  I'm currently reading "IBM and the Holocaust".  They're punch card technology was one of the main reasons that the Nazis were able to keep track of their population, seperate out the Jewish, handicapped, and other "unwanted" people and organize their mass destruction.  Nobody realized this because nobody understood the potential use for such a "computer system" that would allow a government to have instant access to all kinds of information about their population.  Governments today have even better access and use of this type of information, but people know they do now.  Many people are much more cautious now about what information they put out about themselves, and how it is being used by governments... and companies (Grocery store discount cards for example).  <P>Good luck on the paper!  Hope you get an A ||||reg||1
