Z||000015||bHooper||02-22-2001||10:07 PM||||Lisa, I apologize that I don't remember the name of the tribe.  I learned about them on a  older documentary that was either on AMC or A&E 2 weekends ago, early in the morning (between 7 and 9 am EST).  I wish I could remember.<P>i found this website while looking for info on the tribe... bery interesting!<BR> <A HREF="http://kurellian.tripod.com/lostcv1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://kurellian.tripod.com/lostcv1.html</A> <P>here's a link to a south pacific studies <P>virtual library<BR> <A HREF="http://coombs.anu.edu.au/WWWVL-PacificStudies.html" TARGET=_blank>http://coombs.anu.edu.au/WWWVL-PacificStudies.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.lib.berkeley.edu/MRC/IndigenousVid.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lib.berkeley.edu/MRC/IndigenousVid.html</A> <P>i'm sorry, Lisa, I'm a knowledge junky... just try to play me in trivial pursuit, when it comes to useless knowledge!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000016||frontal lobe||02-23-2001||12:08 PM||||History is great, if you are talking about understanding the concepts and not just knowing dates.<P>History should be a good teacher to help you not make mistakes in the future.  See what others have done that didn't turn out well, try to understand why, and then don't try that idea again.<P>History also helps you understand why things are done today the way they are.  Examples:<BR>Why did the supreme court decide abortion was ok in 1973 when it had never been up to that point?  What was going on in our society then?<P>Why are the labor laws what they are today?  What had employers done to employees in the past?<P>Microsoft just went through a big anti-trust trial.  What happened in our country's history with monopolies in the past?<P>Where did the idea of presidential pardons come from?  What is the history behind that?<P>"Separation of church and state".  Where in history did that start?  Why?  What was the historical intent of the constitution regarding this?<P>Electoral college vs. popular vote.  Why do we use an electoral college?  What was the history behind the thinking that led to this system?<P>Indian gambling casinos.  What is the rich historical heritage of the Indian culture that led to these being allowed now?<P>Understanding the thought processes involved in historical context should help us decide if the reason we do things is based on timeless principles or was it a reaction to a certain circumstance for that period of time?<P>Looking at history should also make us look at how people have lived through most of time and then see how good we have it, and then be appreciative instead of such a country of whiners and crybabies.<P>So we can look at current decisions in a thoughtful historical context, or we can all just be sheep and follow along.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Songman||02-25-2001||01:22 PM||||Just got home and found this subject...<P>My summary of the value of history is that if a civilization is intelligent enough they can learn from the mistakes (and the triumphs) or the past to make their society better.<P>And for my personal observations... I think that a lot of young people these days have no idea about history and that is a scary thing. All of the skin heads running around screaming HIEL makes me wonder if 50 years is all it takes to forget something so horrible.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Jake_Dragon||02-25-2001||10:21 PM||||History is a collection of knowledge that one can not mass in ones lifetime. Imagine if everyone had to start from scratch and learn everything for oneself. How many of us would be pushing square wheeled carts? Or replacing ones fuel pump when by doing a little reading if would be apparent that it was just a relay.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000019||MrPBody||02-26-2001||08:35 AM||||For me, History's value lies in its perspective on the present.  Study of history helps us understand the likely effect of our current actions.<P>After many years, I am re-reading Barbara Tuchman's <I>A Distant Mirror</I>, possibly because I recently read Michael Crichton's <I>Timeline</I>.  Both books persuade me that I don't want to visit the 14th century!<BR>||||reg||1
